; S1 fix (M2-fixes Round 3): the F1 CLI regression test in surface.ml
; shells out to the built `tot` CLI (see [expect_cli_run_lines]), and
; plain OCaml module dependencies (what dune's build graph actually
; tracks) cannot express "rebuild ../bin/tot.exe before this test runs":
; the two executables share no library, so nothing in the module graph
; ties them together. The (deps ...) field on a `tests` stanza only
; feeds the generated `runtest` alias action, not `dune exec`/`dune
; build` of the exe target itself (verified empirically: adding a bare
; `(deps ../bin/tot.exe)` here did NOT rebuild a stale tot.exe under
; `dune exec test/surface.exe` after touching lib/interp.ml). This rule
; generates a real OCaml module that DOES depend on ../bin/tot.exe;
; test/surface.ml references it (see [Tot_exe_dep.tot_built]), which
; forces dune to link it into surface.exe, which forces dune to run
; this rule first, which forces ../bin/tot.exe to be rebuilt first.
(rule
 (targets tot_exe_dep.ml)
 (deps ../bin/tot.exe)
 (action
  (write-file tot_exe_dep.ml
   "(* generated by test/dune's rule; do not edit. Depending on\n   ../bin/tot.exe above is the build-time tripwire described there:\n   it forces dune to rebuild the tot CLI before test/surface.exe can\n   even compile. [tot_built] itself carries no information; its only\n   job is to exist so test/surface.ml has something to reference,\n   which is what makes the module dependency (and therefore the exe\n   dependency) real instead of an unused file sitting next to the\n   real one. *)\nlet tot_built : unit = ()\n")))

(tests
 (names main surface)
 (libraries tot_kernel tot_surface))
